statement:
Let n >= 1, l >= 0, p > n, mu a measure on R^(n+l), H : R^(n+l) -> R^(n+l), w : R^(n+l) -> R, y in R^(n+l), 0 < R < S and Lambda > 0. Write r = |x - y| and kappa = (p - n)/(4p). Assume:
- mu(B_S(y)) < infinity;
- w is mu-a.e. strongly measurable with 0 <= w <= 1 mu-a.e.;
- H is mu-integrable on B_S(y), and integral_{B_S(y)} |H|^p dmu < infinity;
- (R^(p-n) integral_{B_R(y)} |H|^p dmu)^(1/p) <= kappa Lambda;
- for every C^1 function phi with phi = 1 on (-infinity, 1] and phi = 0 on [T, infinity), and every rho > 0 with T rho <= S: integral [n phi(r/rho) + (r/rho) phi'(r/rho)(1 - w)] dmu = - integral phi(r/rho)(x - y).H dmu.
Then there is F : R -> R such that:
(i) for 0 < r <= R: e^(-Lambda) <= F(r) <= e^Lambda, |F(r) - 1| <= (1/2) e^(Lambda/2) Lambda (r/R)^(1-n/p), and |F(r) - 1| <= Lambda (r/R)^(1-n/p) if Lambda <= 1;
(ii) for 0 < s <= r <= R: w/|x - y|^n is mu-integrable on B_r(y) minus B_s(y), and e^(-Lambda) A <= Phi(r) - Phi(s) <= e^Lambda A, where A = integral_{B_r(y) minus B_s(y)} w/|x - y|^n dmu and Phi(t) = F(t) t^(-n) mu(B_t(y)) + F(t) - 1.
